Organising a yoga retreat always starts with the same question: where? The venue is half the battle. You need peace and quiet, space, light – a place where participants feel cut off from everyday life the moment they arrive. The Château de la Huberdière, in Touraine, hosts yoga retreats and wellness courses on a private 14-hectare estate, including an enclosed park.

Here’s what we’ve learnt from organising these trips, and what really matters to an organiser.

## Choosing a venue for a yoga retreat

A good yoga retreat needs three things: a spacious practice area, comfortable rooms, and silence. The rest is just a bonus. La Huberdière brings these three elements together in one place, which avoids the need for travel and keeps the group together from morning to night.

The estate is entirely private. You are not staying in a hotel shared with other guests: the château, its reception rooms and grounds are all yours for the duration of the course. This exclusivity makes all the difference when it comes to a group’s focus. Details regarding capacity and organisation can be found on the [retreat] page (/retraite).

## A peaceful château in the heart of Touraine

The château is set within a walled, wooded park in Nazelles-Négron, near Amboise. Here, you can hear the wind in the trees, not the traffic. It is this silence that allows a meditation session or a morning workout to take centre stage.

The Touraine region adds its mild climate and the landscapes of the Loire Valley. Between sessions, participants take a stroll in the park, read in the sunshine, or head off to the nearby banks of the Loire. This natural setting is part of the treatment: it soothes the mind even before the first pose.

## The practice area: the Orangery and the park

The heart of a retreat is the space where activities take place. The Orangerie offers 200 m² of light-filled space that is fully adaptable: open-plan for a yoga mat per person, or set up with tables for a writing workshop. The four more intimate lounges are ideal for small groups, meetings or quiet reflection.

As soon as the weather turns fine, the park becomes a studio: sunrise yoga on the lawn, talking circles under the trees, meditative walks across the fourteen hectares. You can adapt the layout to suit your discipline, and we’ll prepare the rooms accordingly.

## Accommodation and catering for the group

When it comes to a retreat, the logistics make all the difference between a smooth-running retreat and a tiring one. The château can accommodate up to 20 participants in ten suites, each with an en-suite bathroom, with rooms available for single or shared occupancy depending on the nature of the stay.

Meals are just as important as yoga on a retreat. I prepare seasonal dishes, which can be adapted to vegetarian or gluten-free diets, using produce from the market and the vegetable garden. Full details of the meals can be found on the [catering] page (/restauration). Staying, practising and eating in the same place helps to maintain focus: the rhythm of the course is sustained, from the moment you wake up to the final session.

## Beyond yoga: meditation, writing, personal development

The venue is ideal for all forms of gentle practice, not just yoga. Meditation and mindfulness find a rare haven in the tranquillity of the estate. Writers’ residencies and workshops make the most of the Orangery and the cosy lounges. Personal development seminars, digital detox retreats and creative workshops – whether in painting, theatre or music masterclasses – are all well suited to this setting.

This versatility attracts a wide range of organisers, and many return year after year. This is the best indication that a venue has found its place in the retreat calendar.

## Accessibility for participants

A remote location doesn’t have to be difficult to reach. The château is about 2 hours and 20 minutes from Paris by road, or an hour by TGV to Saint-Pierre-des-Corps, followed by a thirty-minute drive. Far enough away to offer a change of scenery, yet close enough for participants to attend without wasting a whole day. We can help organise arrivals and, if required, shuttle services from the station.

## What format and duration should a retreat have?

The format should be chosen according to your audience and your aims. The weekend programme, from Friday evening to Sunday, is suitable for introductory retreats and for groups travelling from afar for just a few days: two nights, four to five sessions, a genuine break from the daily routine. A full week allows for more in-depth work, with a structured programme running from Monday to Sunday and days that alternate between practice, rest and time in nature.

In between, a long weekend of three nights is often the best compromise: long enough to get into a rhythm, yet short enough to remain accessible. As for group size, ten to fifteen participants maintain an intimate atmosphere, whilst up to twenty are suitable for a more intensive retreat. We tailor the accommodation, meals and rooms to each of these set-ups.

## Planning for your retirement

Whether you’re planning a weekend or a week-long stay, we tailor your stay to suit your itinerary: facilities, rooms, meals and timings. You’re in charge of the details; we’ll take care of the rest.

